WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.437
A similarity score is useful, but it should never be the only thing you inspect.

00:00:06.437 --> 00:00:11.622
Look at the rank, the source title, the actual retrieved text, and the score together.

00:00:11.622 --> 00:00:19.242
For our internship question, the first result should contain direct evidence about internship registration.

00:00:19.242 --> 00:00:25.547
A second result about advising may still be related, but it is less specific.

00:00:25.547 --> 00:00:29.789
A third result with a low score may simply be noise.

00:00:30.281 --> 00:00:34.053
The exact numbers on your computer may differ from the example.

00:00:34.053 --> 00:00:39.128
Different embedding models or preprocessing choices can change score ranges.

00:00:39.128 --> 00:00:47.493
Do not build a production rule such as 'anything above point eight is always good' without evaluating your own data.

00:00:47.493 --> 00:00:50.738
First ask whether the ranking makes sense.

00:00:50.738 --> 00:00:55.918
Then collect evaluation questions and study the score distribution.

00:00:55.918 --> 00:01:00.262
Retrieval quality is about evidence, not just numbers.
